WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.441 --> 00:00:04.578
- Our minds are designed
to stop you at all costs

00:00:04.578 --> 00:00:08.278
from doing anything that might hurt you.

00:00:08.278 --> 00:00:10.711
If you notice that you
daydream about being a writer,

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:11.716
if you notice that you daydream

00:00:11.716 --> 00:00:13.038
about starting a tech company,

00:00:13.038 --> 00:00:16.055
if you notice you daydream
about building bridges,

00:00:16.055 --> 00:00:17.737
follow your curiosity.

00:00:17.737 --> 00:00:20.956
So if it were easy to get
what you wanted in life,

00:00:20.956 --> 00:00:23.197
Google would have all
ready figured out an app.

00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:25.437
- She's an American
speaker, TV personality,

00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:27.086
coach, and author.

00:00:27.086 --> 00:00:29.861
Her TEDx talk on how to
stop screwing yourself over

00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:32.951
has over 10 million views on YouTube.

00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:36.422
Her book, Stop Saying You're
Fine is a business best seller.

00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:38.901
She's Mel Robbins, and
here's my take on her top 10

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:39.940
rules to success.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.519
Also guys, as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:41.519 --> 00:00:43.675
that really resonates with
you, please leave it down

00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:45.444
in the comments below,
put quotes around it so

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:47.662
other people can be
inspired by your sharing,

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:50.125
and also when you write it
down, you're much more likely

00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:52.455
to lock it in for yourself too.

00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:53.293
Enjoy.

00:00:53.293 --> 00:00:56.352
(intense deep music)

00:00:56.352 --> 00:00:59.769
(strong inspiring music)

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:10.496
- We bought into this complete
falsehood that at some

00:01:11.486 --> 00:01:14.206
point, you're going to have
the courage, at some point

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:15.184
you're going to have the confidence,

00:01:15.184 --> 00:01:16.725
and it's total bull (beep) frankly.

00:01:16.725 --> 00:01:17.866
Are we allowed to swear on this show?

00:01:17.866 --> 00:01:18.904
- Absolutely.
- Okay.

00:01:18.904 --> 00:01:22.774
It's complete garbage and
so there are so many people

00:01:22.774 --> 00:01:25.842
in the world and, you know,
you may be watching this

00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:29.591
right now and you have
those incredible ideas,

00:01:29.591 --> 00:01:32.505
and what you think is
missing is motivation,

00:01:32.505 --> 00:01:35.330
and that's not true because
the way that our minds are

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.107
wired, and the fact that
human beings is that we

00:01:39.107 --> 00:01:43.022
are not designed to do things
that are uncomfortable,

00:01:43.022 --> 00:01:44.939
or scary, or difficult.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:49.232
Our brains are designed to
protect us from those things,

00:01:49.232 --> 00:01:51.778
because our brains are
trying to keep us alive.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.123
And in order to change, in
order to build a business,

00:01:54.123 --> 00:01:56.992
in order to be the best
parent, the best spouse,

00:01:56.992 --> 00:01:59.973
to do all those things that
you know you want to do with

00:01:59.973 --> 00:02:02.790
your life, with your
work, with your dreams,

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.628
you're going to have to do
things that are difficult,

00:02:05.628 --> 00:02:07.498
uncertain, or scary.

00:02:07.498 --> 00:02:10.487
Which sets up this problem for all of us,

00:02:10.487 --> 00:02:13.229
you're never going to feel
like it, motivation's garbage.

00:02:13.229 --> 00:02:17.485
You only feel motivated to
do the things that are easy,

00:02:17.485 --> 00:02:18.466
right?

00:02:18.466 --> 00:02:19.856
- Why do you think that is?

00:02:19.856 --> 00:02:22.974
- Oh, I know exactly why
that is because I've studied

00:02:22.974 --> 00:02:25.755
this so much because, for
me, one of the hardest things

00:02:25.755 --> 00:02:29.922
to figure out was why is it so
hard to do the little things

00:02:33.455 --> 00:02:34.895
that would improve my life.

00:02:34.895 --> 00:02:38.462
And what I've come to realize,
and what we'll talk a lot

00:02:38.462 --> 00:02:42.640
about today, is that the way
that our minds are designed

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.361
is our minds are designed
to stop you at all costs

00:02:47.361 --> 00:02:51.150
from doing anything that might hurt you,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:56.037
and the way that this all
happens is it all starts

00:02:56.037 --> 00:03:00.427
with something super subtle,
that none of us ever catch,

00:03:00.427 --> 00:03:03.299
and that is with this
habit that all of us have

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:04.918
that nobody's talking about.

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:07.751
We all have a habit of hesitating.

00:03:09.479 --> 00:03:12.687
We have an idea, you're
sitting in a meeting,

00:03:12.687 --> 00:03:15.546
you have this incredible
idea, and instead of just,

00:03:15.546 --> 00:03:19.527
you know, saying it, you
stop and you hesitate.

00:03:19.527 --> 00:03:21.658
Now, what none of us realize
is that when you hesitate,

00:03:21.658 --> 00:03:23.809
just that moment, just that micro moment,

00:03:23.809 --> 00:03:26.796
that small hesitation, it
sends a stress signal to your

00:03:26.796 --> 00:03:29.447
brain, it wakes your
brain up and your brain

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:31.635
goes all of a sudden
goes oh, wait a minute,

00:03:31.635 --> 00:03:32.468
why is he hesitating?

00:03:32.468 --> 00:03:35.204
He didn't hesitate when he put
on his killer spiky sneakers,

00:03:35.204 --> 00:03:37.754
he didn't hesitate with the
really cool track pants,

00:03:37.754 --> 00:03:39.135
he didn't hesitate with the NASA teacher.

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:41.792
Now he's hesitating to
talk, something must be up.

00:03:41.792 --> 00:03:44.403
So then your brain goes
to work to protect you.

00:03:44.403 --> 00:03:46.885
It has a million different
ways to protect you,

00:03:46.885 --> 00:03:48.525
one of them is called
the spotlight effect,

00:03:48.525 --> 00:03:51.985
it's a known phenomenon where
your brain magnified risk.

00:03:51.985 --> 00:03:54.707
Why, to pull you away from
something that it perceives

00:03:54.707 --> 00:03:55.827
to be a problem.

00:03:55.827 --> 00:03:59.910
And so, you can truly
trace every single problem,

00:04:01.464 --> 00:04:05.272
or complaint, in your life
to silence and hesitation.

00:04:05.272 --> 00:04:06.683
Those are decisions.

00:04:06.683 --> 00:04:09.941
And what I do, and what's changed my life,

00:04:09.941 --> 00:04:12.979
is waking up and realizing
that motivation's garbage,

00:04:12.979 --> 00:04:15.588
I'm never going to feel like
doing the things that are tough,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.570
or difficult, or uncertain,
or scary, or new,

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.494
so I need to stop waiting
until I feel like it.

00:04:20.494 --> 00:04:24.415
And number two, I am one
decision away from a totally

00:04:24.415 --> 00:04:27.191
different marriage, a
totally different life,

00:04:27.191 --> 00:04:29.866
a totally different job, a
totally different income,

00:04:29.866 --> 00:04:33.776
a totally different
relationship with my kids.

00:04:33.776 --> 00:04:37.235
Not like one decision, I'm divorcing you,

00:04:37.235 --> 00:04:41.143
in the marriage example, but
one decision on, you know,

00:04:41.143 --> 00:04:43.384
you could be having a
conversation with your spouse

00:04:43.384 --> 00:04:45.606
and you feel your emotions rise up,

00:04:45.606 --> 00:04:49.773
and within a tiny window,
those emotions can take over

00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:54.296
and can impact how your marriage goes.

00:04:54.296 --> 00:04:58.463
Or you can learn how to take
control of that micro moment,

00:05:00.487 --> 00:05:03.367
and make a decision to
act in a way that actually

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:04.424
shifts your marriage.

00:05:04.424 --> 00:05:06.728
Your life comes down to your decisions,

00:05:06.728 --> 00:05:09.137
and if you change your decisions,

00:05:09.137 --> 00:05:10.507
you will change everything.

00:05:10.507 --> 00:05:13.137
When I was 22, right after
I graduated from Dartmouth,

00:05:13.137 --> 00:05:16.884
I was so focused on making everyone happy,

00:05:16.884 --> 00:05:20.461
that I almost chose the
worst possible career

00:05:20.461 --> 00:05:21.500
for my personality.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.380
It's not a bad career,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.670
it just could've been horrendous for me.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.792
I was interested in the
environment, so I decided,

00:05:27.792 --> 00:05:29.628
oh yeah, I'll go get a law degree.

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:32.336
And then I'll go work in
Washington, and I think maybe

00:05:32.336 --> 00:05:33.877
I'll work on environmental
policy, and I'd probably

00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:36.217
end up in some cube farm.

00:05:36.217 --> 00:05:39.748
And everyone was thrilled
with the plan, everyone,

00:05:39.748 --> 00:05:41.221
it turns out, but me.

00:05:41.221 --> 00:05:44.657
So here I am, I'm driving
a U-Haul across country to

00:05:44.657 --> 00:05:47.990
go to law school and to get a
degree in environmental law,

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.990
and something started to gnaw at me.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.861
And with every single mile that I drove,

00:05:55.861 --> 00:05:58.691
my thoughts were getting louder,

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.740
and doubts were starting to pour in.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:05.426
My gut was telling me, Mel,
turn the damn U-Haul around,

00:06:05.426 --> 00:06:08.595
but the problem was that
everything was all ready in place.

00:06:08.595 --> 00:06:09.997
I mean, I'm all ready in the U-Haul,

00:06:09.997 --> 00:06:12.803
the thing is all ready
packed, I'm all ready 10 miles

00:06:12.803 --> 00:06:15.764
into my drive, the
tuition had been paid for

00:06:15.764 --> 00:06:18.336
the first semester, I had signed a lease.

00:06:18.336 --> 00:06:21.495
I mean, I could not undo these things.

00:06:21.495 --> 00:06:25.811
Isn't that why it's so difficult
for you to make changes

00:06:25.811 --> 00:06:27.300
in your life?

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.542
Because you think that
things can't be undone?

00:06:30.542 --> 00:06:33.962
The fact is any excuse you
come up with you can undo.

00:06:33.962 --> 00:06:36.869
Tuition can be reimbursed,
apartments can be leased,

00:06:36.869 --> 00:06:38.481
plan B can be invented.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:40.858
So I got up, I repacked that U-Haul,

00:06:40.858 --> 00:06:42.791
and I drove out of town.

00:06:42.791 --> 00:06:45.830
I let myself make a U turn in life.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:48.698
If you ever find your
gut battling your head,

00:06:48.698 --> 00:06:50.050
save yourself the drama.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.139
I guarantee you your gut is right.

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:56.102
Guarantee you your excuses can be undone,

00:06:56.102 --> 00:06:59.681
and I guarantee you you can
make a U turn in your life

00:06:59.681 --> 00:07:01.802
right now if you want to.

00:07:01.802 --> 00:07:05.969
You can be asking for assignments
that are above your level.

00:07:07.269 --> 00:07:09.039
So there's work that you do at your level,

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:11.757
and then there's work that
you do above your level.

00:07:11.757 --> 00:07:14.587
And the only way that you're
going to be promoted to do

00:07:14.587 --> 00:07:17.488
work above your level is
A, if you can demonstrate

00:07:17.488 --> 00:07:20.157
that you're capable of doing
the work at your level,

00:07:20.157 --> 00:07:24.701
and B, if you are proactive
in seeking out opportunities

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:28.468
that expand your capability
and give you a chance to

00:07:28.468 --> 00:07:31.182
work above the level that you're at.

00:07:31.182 --> 00:07:34.791
Nothing's going to happen unless
you take that initial action.

00:07:34.791 --> 00:07:37.591
Nothing's going to happen,
nothing's going to change

00:07:37.591 --> 00:07:39.398
unless you put down
that pen in the meeting

00:07:39.398 --> 00:07:41.924
and you sit up and you pay attention,

00:07:41.924 --> 00:07:44.913
and you listen for your
opportunity to speak.

00:07:44.913 --> 00:07:48.311
Nothing's going to happen unless
you push yourself to go in

00:07:48.311 --> 00:07:51.771
and make the ask for that
assignment that scares you

00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:52.604
to death.

00:07:52.604 --> 00:07:55.411
One night Chris had gone to
bed, I'd been struggling,

00:07:55.411 --> 00:07:57.253
struggling, struggling.

00:07:57.253 --> 00:08:00.097
We still had all the same
problems, we still had a lean

00:08:00.097 --> 00:08:02.001
on the house, still facing bankruptcy,

00:08:02.001 --> 00:08:04.091
still fighting like crazy,
I was still unemployed,

00:08:04.091 --> 00:08:07.924
they still hadn't figured
out the solution yet

00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:12.645
for the business, and I was
about to turn off the TV,

00:08:12.645 --> 00:08:16.355
and there on the TV, there
was this rocket launching,

00:08:16.355 --> 00:08:19.355
and I though oh my gosh, that is it!

00:08:21.967 --> 00:08:26.466
I am going to launch myself
out of bed like a rocket ship,

00:08:26.466 --> 00:08:28.472
like NASA, right here!

00:08:28.472 --> 00:08:31.665
Had launched me out of that bed,
and I'm going to move so fast

00:08:31.665 --> 00:08:33.885
that I don't think.

00:08:33.885 --> 00:08:35.203
I'm going to beat my brain.

00:08:35.203 --> 00:08:38.370
Now here's a really interesting point,

00:08:39.725 --> 00:08:43.804
I talk a lot about your
instincts and inner wisdom.

00:08:43.804 --> 00:08:46.704
And we can get into
this a little bit later,

00:08:46.704 --> 00:08:48.376
but a lot of us talk about
the fact that you have a gut

00:08:48.376 --> 00:08:51.163
feeling, but what all this
research that I've done

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:53.705
for the book and all
the speaking that I do,

00:08:53.705 --> 00:08:56.396
what I've discovered that's
fascinating is actually,

00:08:56.396 --> 00:08:59.287
when you set goals, when
you have an intention on

00:08:59.287 --> 00:09:00.947
something that you want
to change about your life,

00:09:00.947 --> 00:09:02.564
your brain helps you.

00:09:02.564 --> 00:09:05.885
What it does is it opens up
a checklist and then your

00:09:05.885 --> 00:09:09.763
brain goes to work trying
to remind you of that

00:09:09.763 --> 00:09:11.516
intention that you set.

00:09:11.516 --> 00:09:14.433
And it's really important
to develop this skill,

00:09:14.433 --> 00:09:17.877
and I say that work purposefully,
the skill of knowing

00:09:17.877 --> 00:09:22.474
how to hear that inner wisdom
and that intention kicking in,

00:09:22.474 --> 00:09:24.726
and leaning into it quickly.

00:09:24.726 --> 00:09:27.645
So for me, my brain saying
that's it, right there,

00:09:27.645 --> 00:09:30.105
move as fast as a rocket, Mel.

00:09:30.105 --> 00:09:33.646
I wanted to change my life,
and I think most people that

00:09:33.646 --> 00:09:37.813
are miserable, or that are
really like dying to be great,

00:09:38.855 --> 00:09:41.363
and dying to have more, we want to change,

00:09:41.363 --> 00:09:44.644
we want to live a better
life, we want to create more

00:09:44.644 --> 00:09:47.401
for our families, we want to be happier.

00:09:47.401 --> 00:09:50.832
The desire is there, again,
it's about how do you

00:09:50.832 --> 00:09:52.426
from knowledge to action.

00:09:52.426 --> 00:09:54.434
So the first thing in this
story that's important

00:09:54.434 --> 00:09:57.385
is realizing that the answer was in me,

00:09:57.385 --> 00:10:00.725
and my mind was telling me pay attention.

00:10:00.725 --> 00:10:01.777
It could have also been the bourbon.

00:10:01.777 --> 00:10:02.664
(people laughing)

00:10:02.664 --> 00:10:04.946
I had a couple manhattans that night.

00:10:04.946 --> 00:10:07.985
Anyway, the next morning,
the alarm goes off and

00:10:07.985 --> 00:10:10.235
I pretended NASA was there.

00:10:12.021 --> 00:10:13.967
It's the stupidest story,
I literally went five,

00:10:13.967 --> 00:10:15.697
four, three, two, one.

00:10:15.697 --> 00:10:18.167
I counted out loud and then I stood up.

00:10:18.167 --> 00:10:21.263
I'll never forget standing
there in my bedroom,

00:10:21.263 --> 00:10:25.430
it was dark, it was cold,
it was winter in Boston.

00:10:27.343 --> 00:10:29.102
And for the first time in three months,

00:10:29.102 --> 00:10:33.483
I had beaten my habit of
hitting the snooze button.

00:10:33.483 --> 00:10:37.143
I couldn't believe it, and
I thought wait a minute,

00:10:37.143 --> 00:10:39.831
counting backwards, that
is the dumbest thing

00:10:39.831 --> 00:10:41.621
I've ever heard in my entire life.

00:10:41.621 --> 00:10:43.662
Well the next morning I
used it again, it worked.

00:10:43.662 --> 00:10:45.502
The next morning I used
it again and it worked.

00:10:45.502 --> 00:10:48.022
The next morning I used
it again and it worked.

00:10:48.022 --> 00:10:51.360
And then I started to notice
something really interesting,

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.193
there were moments all
day long, all day long,

00:10:56.332 --> 00:11:00.201
just like that five second moment in bed,

00:11:00.201 --> 00:11:03.534
where I knew knowledge what I should do.

00:11:05.051 --> 00:11:08.070
And if I didn't move within five seconds,

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.689
my brain would step in
and talk me out of it.

00:11:11.689 --> 00:11:13.498
Every human being has
a five second window,

00:11:13.498 --> 00:11:15.196
it might even be shorter for you.

00:11:15.196 --> 00:11:19.494
You have about a five second
window in which you can

00:11:19.494 --> 00:11:23.543
move from idea to action
before your brain kicks into

00:11:23.543 --> 00:11:27.460
full gear and sabotages
any change in behavior.

00:11:29.032 --> 00:11:31.211
Because remember, your
brain is wired to stop you

00:11:31.211 --> 00:11:33.314
from doing things that are
uncomfortable, or uncertain,

00:11:33.314 --> 00:11:34.954
or scary.

00:11:34.954 --> 00:11:39.073
It's your job to learn how
to move from those ideas

00:11:39.073 --> 00:11:43.055
that could change everything
into acting on them.

00:11:43.055 --> 00:11:47.615
I think you have to be really
in tune with your impulses

00:11:47.615 --> 00:11:50.976
and with your natural state of curiosity.

00:11:50.976 --> 00:11:53.467
And your curiosity, and the
things that you're naturally

00:11:53.467 --> 00:11:56.404
drawn toward, that's the guide.

00:11:56.404 --> 00:11:59.266
If you could spend hours
singing, if you could spend hours

00:11:59.266 --> 00:12:01.536
baking, if you could spend hours writing,

00:12:01.536 --> 00:12:03.715
if you could spend hours learning code,

00:12:03.715 --> 00:12:06.662
that's a huge signal that
that's something that you're

00:12:06.662 --> 00:12:09.204
really innately interested in.

00:12:09.204 --> 00:12:11.561
And so if you're curious about something,

00:12:11.561 --> 00:12:13.613
if you notice that you
daydream about being a writer,

00:12:13.613 --> 00:12:16.365
if you notice that you
daydream about starting a tech

00:12:16.365 --> 00:12:18.724
company, if you notice you
daydream about building bridges,

00:12:18.724 --> 00:12:22.515
follow your curiosity, and you
can do that a number of ways.

00:12:22.515 --> 00:12:23.926
First of all, Google it.

00:12:23.926 --> 00:12:27.663
There are millions and
millions of pages out there on

00:12:27.663 --> 00:12:30.593
the web that are related to
what you're interested in.

00:12:30.593 --> 00:12:32.474
Secondly, find an online course.

00:12:32.474 --> 00:12:35.083
Most of the major universities
are offering their courses

00:12:35.083 --> 00:12:36.113
online for free.

00:12:36.113 --> 00:12:38.683
Third, buy a book about it.

00:12:38.683 --> 00:12:41.112
Put yourself in a collision
course with the thing that

00:12:41.112 --> 00:12:44.772
you're curious about, and you
will get closer and closer

00:12:44.772 --> 00:12:46.531
and closer to figuring it out.

00:12:46.531 --> 00:12:49.739
- What about when someone
can't realize or identify

00:12:49.739 --> 00:12:52.068
their own negative feelings,
someone that has been

00:12:52.068 --> 00:12:54.258
conditioned to (mumbles)
negative feelings and

00:12:54.258 --> 00:12:55.720
believes that that's normal.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.000
This question comes from Basana Denigaga.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.190
- It is your normal, you're
right, it's a really...

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:06.588
First of all, I'm sorry that
that's been your experience

00:13:06.588 --> 00:13:09.226
of life because that really stinks,

00:13:09.226 --> 00:13:10.756
and it doesn't have to be that way.

00:13:10.756 --> 00:13:13.626
But here's where I want you
to go, it's interesting about

00:13:13.626 --> 00:13:18.456
what you said is the fact that
these feelings are normal.

00:13:18.456 --> 00:13:20.615
Normal just means you're used to them,

00:13:20.615 --> 00:13:23.486
but you don't have to live with them.

00:13:23.486 --> 00:13:26.953
So even though you're so used
to these negative feelings,

00:13:26.953 --> 00:13:30.674
and these feelings of doubt,
you still don't like them,

00:13:30.674 --> 00:13:33.784
you still have the ability to stop that.

00:13:33.784 --> 00:13:36.025
So I want you to think of
a moment in time when you

00:13:36.025 --> 00:13:38.594
were really happy, like
it could be a memory from

00:13:38.594 --> 00:13:40.362
being little, it could be
something that you dream

00:13:40.362 --> 00:13:41.325
of doing.

00:13:41.325 --> 00:13:43.362
And I want you to come up
with something really specific

00:13:43.362 --> 00:13:45.294
about that moment.

00:13:45.294 --> 00:13:46.447
Like, think about what you were wearing,

00:13:46.447 --> 00:13:49.153
or think about the blue sky.

00:13:49.153 --> 00:13:52.598
That's going to be where
I pointer anchor thought.

00:13:52.598 --> 00:13:56.455
Every time, from this moment
forward, that you wake up,

00:13:56.455 --> 00:14:00.237
or you find yourself drifting
to those normal feelings

00:14:00.237 --> 00:14:04.404
of I'm a loser, I'm worthless,
I'm never going to find love.

00:14:07.041 --> 00:14:10.119
They may be things that
you're used to thinking,

00:14:10.119 --> 00:14:14.657
but that doesn't matter
because you can change them.

00:14:14.657 --> 00:14:18.868
The second that you feel
that, you've drifted over into

00:14:18.868 --> 00:14:20.633
that thought, and I keep
coming over here because

00:14:20.633 --> 00:14:22.396
when you start using that (mumbles),

00:14:22.396 --> 00:14:24.205
you're going to start to
literally feel which part

00:14:24.205 --> 00:14:25.906
of the mind you're using.

00:14:25.906 --> 00:14:28.382
The worries kind of happen here.

00:14:28.382 --> 00:14:30.207
I want you to five, four, three, two, one,

00:14:30.207 --> 00:14:32.152
and then I want you to insert
this vision of yourself,

00:14:32.152 --> 00:14:36.319
this vision of yourself
where you felt really good.

00:14:37.498 --> 00:14:40.011
And if you can't think of one, invent one.

00:14:40.011 --> 00:14:41.632
How would you feel if you were in love?

00:14:41.632 --> 00:14:43.452
How would you feel if you loved yourself?

00:14:43.452 --> 00:14:45.651
How would you feel if you
did that thing that you're

00:14:45.651 --> 00:14:47.533
scared of that you keep
talking yourself out of it?

00:14:47.533 --> 00:14:48.964
That's your anchor thought.

00:14:48.964 --> 00:14:51.063
Now, when you first start using the rule,

00:14:51.063 --> 00:14:54.653
particularly when you're breaking a habit,

00:14:54.653 --> 00:14:57.831
a habit of thinking negative thoughts.

00:14:57.831 --> 00:15:00.509
When you first start doing
it, you may need to use

00:15:00.509 --> 00:15:03.507
the five second rule 73 times in one day.

00:15:03.507 --> 00:15:07.117
That's okay, I had to do it
like that when I was first

00:15:07.117 --> 00:15:09.857
using the rule to cure myself of anxiety.

00:15:09.857 --> 00:15:12.110
When I would start to
worry about anything,

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:15.567
I'd start to worry about my
kids, or I'd start to worry

00:15:15.567 --> 00:15:18.745
about money, and I'd
feel my thoughts drift,

00:15:18.745 --> 00:15:20.751
and then I'd start to feel my heart race.

00:15:20.751 --> 00:15:23.651
I would stabilize my thoughts,
five, four, three, two, one.

00:15:23.651 --> 00:15:27.142
I'd activate my prefrontal
cortex, lower the worries,

00:15:27.142 --> 00:15:29.435
you know, on the other side,
and then I would start to

00:15:29.435 --> 00:15:32.934
think about something that
actually made me feel really calm

00:15:32.934 --> 00:15:34.311
or excited.

00:15:34.311 --> 00:15:36.533
That is the battle of your mind.

00:15:36.533 --> 00:15:38.541
And you know, the other thing
about normal that I just

00:15:38.541 --> 00:15:43.307
want to say is that the reason
why it feels normal to doubt

00:15:43.307 --> 00:15:47.740
yourself and to have low
self worth is because you've

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.490
thought this so long.

00:15:51.121 --> 00:15:52.992
It's become a pattern, just
like chewing your nails

00:15:52.992 --> 00:15:56.043
becomes a pattern or behavior
that you don't think about.

00:15:56.043 --> 00:15:58.853
Well, the cool thing is
that based on science,

00:15:58.853 --> 00:16:01.691
you can actually change any pattern,

00:16:01.691 --> 00:16:03.301
even a thinking one.

00:16:03.301 --> 00:16:07.307
So use the five second rule,
every single time you catch

00:16:07.307 --> 00:16:10.780
yourself thinking the nasty
stuff and then activate

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.930
your prefrontal cortex,
and force yourself to think

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.240
something positive.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.731
Every single morning, Monday
through Friday's at 9:00 AM,

00:16:18.731 --> 00:16:23.032
I host a live call in radio
show on Sirius Satellite Radio.

00:16:23.032 --> 00:16:25.611
It's called Make it
Happen with Mel Robbins.

00:16:25.611 --> 00:16:29.019
And the thing that's been
so crazy about that show is

00:16:29.019 --> 00:16:33.656
every single person that
calls me on that show,

00:16:33.656 --> 00:16:37.495
they call in because they're
feeling stuck in their lives,

00:16:37.495 --> 00:16:40.306
and they're resigned about
their ability to change

00:16:40.306 --> 00:16:41.139
their lives.

00:16:41.139 --> 00:16:42.976
And I'm not talking about
people that are nuts,

00:16:42.976 --> 00:16:45.994
I'm talking about successful
people, like you and me,

00:16:45.994 --> 00:16:48.322
that just somehow got
stuck in their lives.

00:16:48.322 --> 00:16:50.175
And I'm not talking about
people that are looking

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:53.131
for cheesy self help, I'm
talking about people that really

00:16:53.131 --> 00:16:55.894
want to figure out how to
move themselves forward.

00:16:55.894 --> 00:16:58.315
And you know, I find that
so many of us think that our

00:16:58.315 --> 00:17:01.203
dreams are unreachable, or unrealistic,

00:17:01.203 --> 00:17:03.083
and it's just so sad.

00:17:03.083 --> 00:17:06.544
I mean, people think that
their dreams disappear,

00:17:06.544 --> 00:17:09.374
and if that's what you
think, congratulations,

00:17:09.374 --> 00:17:11.333
you're officially stuck.

00:17:11.333 --> 00:17:14.622
And the truth is that our
dreams are always there,

00:17:14.622 --> 00:17:18.523
and they don't ever give up
on us, we give up on them.

00:17:18.523 --> 00:17:22.424
But here's the secret, if
you just force yourself,

00:17:22.424 --> 00:17:24.660
and I mean force because I
know damn well none of you

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.420
want to do anything about this,
and I also know you're sitting

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.638
there like, yeah, this self
help crap, this isn't for me.

00:17:30.638 --> 00:17:34.955
But if you just force yourself
to take a couple small steps,

00:17:34.955 --> 00:17:37.735
there's always a surprise.

00:17:37.735 --> 00:17:38.975
So let's talk about Starbucks.

00:17:38.975 --> 00:17:43.142
Starbucks, in 2008, very
interesting thing happened,

00:17:44.405 --> 00:17:47.025
and many of you, if you were
in the financial services

00:17:47.025 --> 00:17:50.056
market then probably know
this, that they're net income

00:17:50.056 --> 00:17:53.953
decreased by about 28% that year.

00:17:53.953 --> 00:17:58.120
At the time, they had 16,000
stores in 44 countries,

00:17:59.261 --> 00:18:02.082
and they had gone through
this massive explosive growth

00:18:02.082 --> 00:18:05.020
segment, in terms of going from public in,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:09.427
I think it was like 1992,
they only had a 125 stores,

00:18:09.427 --> 00:18:12.652
and now here we are
2008, they've got 16,000.

00:18:12.652 --> 00:18:15.434
So what ends up happening
is Howard Schultz,

00:18:15.434 --> 00:18:18.212
who was the chairman of the
board, comes back in as the CEO.

00:18:18.212 --> 00:18:20.331
Very complex situation, right?

00:18:20.331 --> 00:18:22.925
You've got operations in 44 countries,

00:18:22.925 --> 00:18:25.645
you've got massive reductions
in terms of the profits

00:18:25.645 --> 00:18:28.846
that you have, you've got the
stock market in Wall Street

00:18:28.846 --> 00:18:31.663
screaming at you, you've
got customers that are going

00:18:31.663 --> 00:18:34.443
to Dunkin Donuts because there's
a recession that's hitting.

00:18:34.443 --> 00:18:35.276
So what do you do?

00:18:35.276 --> 00:18:38.744
Well, he was very clear,
again, back to the two rules.

00:18:38.744 --> 00:18:41.213
What do I stand for?

00:18:41.213 --> 00:18:43.046
Like, what am I doing?

00:18:43.935 --> 00:18:46.337
And what do I want the outcome to be?

00:18:46.337 --> 00:18:49.807
So, he was very clear that
what he stood for as a leader

00:18:49.807 --> 00:18:51.854
and what Starbucks should stand for is

00:18:51.854 --> 00:18:53.859
the Starbucks experience.

00:18:53.859 --> 00:18:57.073
He wanted to go back to the beginning and

00:18:57.073 --> 00:19:00.346
reintroduce people to the romance
of having a cup of coffee.

00:19:00.346 --> 00:19:01.995
Because you know, that's
how Starbucks launched

00:19:01.995 --> 00:19:05.025
in the beginning, that he
was traveling and was over

00:19:05.025 --> 00:19:06.985
in Italy, and was like, god,
why don't we have places

00:19:06.985 --> 00:19:09.874
like these great stops to
have a little cup of coffee

00:19:09.874 --> 00:19:11.102
over in the United States.

00:19:11.102 --> 00:19:13.882
Boom, Starbucks, an idea,
it's born, it's launched.

00:19:13.882 --> 00:19:16.424
So he decides he's going to
do whatever he can to get

00:19:16.424 --> 00:19:19.493
the romance of having
a cup of coffee back.

00:19:19.493 --> 00:19:23.174
In life, in any situation,
whether you're talking about

00:19:23.174 --> 00:19:25.975
growing your company,
growing your bank account,

00:19:25.975 --> 00:19:29.032
getting in a relationship,
getting out of a relationship,

00:19:29.032 --> 00:19:31.420
getting something done at
the school system that your

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.135
kids are going to, on behalf of your kids.

00:19:34.135 --> 00:19:35.714
Whatever it is that you're up to in life,

00:19:35.714 --> 00:19:38.608
this is the only question
that you need to ask yourself,

00:19:38.608 --> 00:19:39.656
what is it that I want.

00:19:39.656 --> 00:19:42.927
Because the truth of the matter
is if you can answer that,

00:19:42.927 --> 00:19:44.154
you can have it.

00:19:44.154 --> 00:19:47.324
So, if it were easy to get
what you wanted in life,

00:19:47.324 --> 00:19:49.846
Google would have all
ready figured out an app.

00:19:49.846 --> 00:19:51.706
You would just type in,
okay, I'm a student,

00:19:51.706 --> 00:19:54.563
and I want to go from being a
student to being successful.

00:19:54.563 --> 00:19:57.221
And Google would tell you
go to college, then you get

00:19:57.221 --> 00:20:00.175
your masters, then you
land this killer job,

00:20:00.175 --> 00:20:02.536
and then you put in a bunch
of Facetime while you're

00:20:02.536 --> 00:20:06.042
checking Facebook, then
you get promoted to manager

00:20:06.042 --> 00:20:07.868
and you realize management
people kind of stink,

00:20:07.868 --> 00:20:10.670
so then you keep climbing
the corporate ladder.

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:12.838
And then you start to wonder,
well, how did I end up here?

00:20:12.838 --> 00:20:14.621
And then you're like, is
this what success looks like?

00:20:14.621 --> 00:20:16.249
You're like, no, I'm
going to go to J Hilburn,

00:20:16.249 --> 00:20:17.381
that's what success looks like.

00:20:17.381 --> 00:20:20.381
(audience cheering)

00:20:21.578 --> 00:20:24.408
So keep this in mind, if
there's something that you want,

00:20:24.408 --> 00:20:27.099
like seven people, or a trip to Africa,

00:20:27.099 --> 00:20:29.235
or you're going to make it to Mexico,

00:20:29.235 --> 00:20:31.519
in the pink shirt, I'm
looking at you again, sorry.

00:20:31.519 --> 00:20:34.686
I'm watching you, I'm routing for you.

00:20:36.127 --> 00:20:40.628
Keep in mind this, here you
are, this is what you want.

00:20:40.628 --> 00:20:42.676
I spend a lot of time on the cross fields,

00:20:42.676 --> 00:20:45.485
although they don't really look like this.

00:20:45.485 --> 00:20:47.317
You tend to think that what you want,

00:20:47.317 --> 00:20:49.265
you just march right towards it.

00:20:49.265 --> 00:20:52.475
But if in sports, you march
right towards the goal,

00:20:52.475 --> 00:20:53.590
what happens?

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.383
The defenders come in, and
what the defenders are is

00:20:56.383 --> 00:20:58.673
it's your brain saying,
ain't going to happen.

00:20:58.673 --> 00:21:01.291
So the second you start moving
toward that thing you want,

00:21:01.291 --> 00:21:04.052
be ready, you're going to tell
yourself you don't feel like it.

00:21:04.052 --> 00:21:05.941
You're going to have to force
yourself, it's happening,

00:21:05.941 --> 00:21:07.683
there's no way to get around it.

00:21:07.683 --> 00:21:10.411
So what you're going to have to
do is you're going to basically

00:21:10.411 --> 00:21:14.945
get bounced around a
lot, all over the place.

00:21:14.945 --> 00:21:16.576
And there are going to be a
lot of times that you feel

00:21:16.576 --> 00:21:19.885
like this was a really dumb
idea to start this business,

00:21:19.885 --> 00:21:22.376
because it's going to get hard.

00:21:22.376 --> 00:21:24.564
And I want to remind you about something.

00:21:24.564 --> 00:21:28.207
Unlike the sports field where
there's an in and out of

00:21:28.207 --> 00:21:31.165
bounds, the great thing
about life is there actually

00:21:31.165 --> 00:21:32.587
aren't any.

00:21:32.587 --> 00:21:36.166
And it's usually in those
moments when you feel like

00:21:36.166 --> 00:21:39.556
you can't do it, that you're
closer than ever to having

00:21:39.556 --> 00:21:41.484
everything that you wanted.

00:21:41.484 --> 00:21:44.404
And I wanted to show you
this so that you keep in mind

00:21:44.404 --> 00:21:47.201
because I think a lot of
times when you look back,

00:21:47.201 --> 00:21:50.408
it's the darkest moments
where you realize holy cow,

00:21:50.408 --> 00:21:52.321
that's what projected me forward.

00:21:52.321 --> 00:21:56.106
We live in the most
amazing moment in time.

00:21:56.106 --> 00:21:59.784
So that thing that you have
up here, whatever it may be,

00:21:59.784 --> 00:22:02.785
you want to use healthy
eating to cure your diabetes,

00:22:02.785 --> 00:22:07.347
you want to figure out how
to take care of the elders

00:22:07.347 --> 00:22:10.186
and start a new hospice center,
you want to move to Africa

00:22:10.186 --> 00:22:12.454
and build a school, guess what?

00:22:12.454 --> 00:22:16.541
You could walk into a bookstore
right now and buy at least

00:22:16.541 --> 00:22:19.082
10 books written by credentialed experts

00:22:19.082 --> 00:22:20.765
on how the hell you do it.

00:22:20.765 --> 00:22:24.553
You could Google it and you
could probably find at least,

00:22:24.553 --> 00:22:29.275
I don't know, a thousand
blogs documenting the step,

00:22:29.275 --> 00:22:32.386
by step, by step transformation
that somebody else

00:22:32.386 --> 00:22:33.936
is all ready doing.

00:22:33.936 --> 00:22:38.275
You can find anybody online
and cyber stalk them.

00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:40.276
(audience laughing)

00:22:40.276 --> 00:22:42.810
You can just walk in
their footsteps and...

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:44.380
You know, just use the
science of drafting,

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.340
follow whatever everyone else has done,

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:48.673
because someone else
is all ready doing it.

00:22:48.673 --> 00:22:52.277
So why don't you have what
you want when you have

00:22:52.277 --> 00:22:54.361
all the information that you need,

00:22:54.361 --> 00:22:56.311
you have the contacts that you need?

00:22:56.311 --> 00:22:59.321
There are probably free tools
online that allow you to

00:22:59.321 --> 00:23:01.962
start a business, or join
a group, or do whatever

00:23:01.962 --> 00:23:03.462
the heck you want.

00:23:05.312 --> 00:23:07.812
It all comes down to one word.

00:23:11.481 --> 00:23:12.930
(people laughing)

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.620
Shut the front door, you
know what I'm talking about.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:17.199
(audience laughing)

00:23:17.199 --> 00:23:19.532
The F bomb, it's everywhere.

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.859
You hear it all the time, and
I honestly don't understand

00:23:23.859 --> 00:23:26.083
what the appeal is over the word.

00:23:26.083 --> 00:23:27.890
I mean, you don't sound
smart when you say it,

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:32.057
and it's really not expressing
how you really feel.

00:23:34.727 --> 00:23:38.176
It's sort of a cheap,
you know, shock to take,

00:23:38.176 --> 00:23:42.658
and of course you know I'm
talking about the word fine.

00:23:42.658 --> 00:23:44.195
(people laughing)

00:23:44.195 --> 00:23:45.028
How you doing?

00:23:45.028 --> 00:23:45.861
I'm fine.

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:48.993
Oh, really, you are?

00:23:48.993 --> 00:23:52.194
Dragging around those extra
40 pounds, you're fine?

00:23:52.194 --> 00:23:55.439
Feeling like roommates with
your spouse, you're fine?

00:23:55.439 --> 00:23:59.606
You haven't had sex in four
months, you're fine, really?

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.437
I don't think so.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:06.923
But see, here's the deal
with saying that you're fine,

00:24:06.923 --> 00:24:11.050
it's actually genius
because if you're fine,

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:14.070
you don't have to do anything about it.

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:16.910
But when you think about this word fine,

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:19.234
it just makes me so angry.

00:24:19.234 --> 00:24:21.021
I mean, here we are at a
conference about being alive

00:24:21.021 --> 00:24:24.860
and you're going to describe
the experience of being alive

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:28.110
as fine, what a flimsy and feeble word.

00:24:29.879 --> 00:24:31.465
If you're crappy, say you're crappy.

00:24:31.465 --> 00:24:34.016
If you're amazing, say you're amazing.

00:24:34.016 --> 00:24:35.906
Tell the truth.

00:24:35.906 --> 00:24:38.997
And this not only goes
for the social construct.

00:24:38.997 --> 00:24:40.481
Oh, I don't want to burden
you with the fact that I hate

00:24:40.481 --> 00:24:43.024
my life, or hey, I'm
amazing but that would make

00:24:43.024 --> 00:24:43.857
you feel terrible.

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:44.690
(people laughing)

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:48.857
The bigger issue with fine is
that you say it to yourself.

00:24:51.934 --> 00:24:54.982
That thing that you want, I guarantee you,

00:24:54.982 --> 00:24:59.149
you've convinced yourself that
you're fine not having it.

00:25:00.922 --> 00:25:03.311
That's why you're not pushing yourself.

00:25:03.311 --> 00:25:06.062
It's the areas in your
life where you've given up,

00:25:06.062 --> 00:25:08.939
where you've said, oh, I'm
fine, my mind's never going to

00:25:08.939 --> 00:25:11.829
change so I just can't
have that conversation.

00:25:11.829 --> 00:25:13.635
I'm fine, you know, we
got to wait until the kids

00:25:13.635 --> 00:25:15.974
graduate before we get
divorced, so we'll just sleep

00:25:15.974 --> 00:25:18.064
in separate bedrooms.

00:25:18.064 --> 00:25:21.173
I'm fine, I lost my job,
I can barely pay my bills

00:25:21.173 --> 00:25:24.256
but whatever, it's hard to get a job.

00:25:25.465 --> 00:25:29.361
And you know, one of the
reasons why this word also just

00:25:29.361 --> 00:25:33.528
annoys me so much is
scientists have calculated...

00:25:35.968 --> 00:25:37.239
Oh yeah, I'm coming down.

00:25:37.239 --> 00:25:41.406
Scientists have calculated
the odds of you being born.

00:25:46.767 --> 00:25:49.487
That's right, they've
crunched the numbers.

00:25:49.487 --> 00:25:50.345
I see you up there.

00:25:50.345 --> 00:25:51.735
(audience laughing)

00:25:51.735 --> 00:25:53.629
They've crunched the numbers on you,

00:25:53.629 --> 00:25:56.500
yeah, you guys standing up,
you want to sit down for this.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.374
They've crunched the
numbers on you being born,

00:25:59.374 --> 00:26:02.874
and they took into account all of the wars

00:26:03.919 --> 00:26:07.438
and the natural disasters,
and the dinosaurs,

00:26:07.438 --> 00:26:09.799
and everything else.

00:26:09.799 --> 00:26:12.549
And do you realize that the odds,

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:16.862
the odds of you, yeah, right
here, put your computer away,

00:26:16.862 --> 00:26:19.242
stand up for me, Doug.

00:26:19.242 --> 00:26:21.622
(people laughing)

00:26:21.622 --> 00:26:24.583
So the odds of Doug here, turn
around, say hi to everybody.

00:26:24.583 --> 00:26:25.474
- Hello.

00:26:25.474 --> 00:26:30.045
- The odds of Doug, Doug
being born at the moment

00:26:30.045 --> 00:26:34.155
in time he was born to the
parents you were born to,

00:26:34.155 --> 00:26:38.322
with he DNA structure that
you have, one in 400 trillion.

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:44.412
Isn't that amazing?

00:26:44.412 --> 00:26:45.382
- [Doug] I'm so lucky.

00:26:45.382 --> 00:26:47.283
- Yes!
(audience laughing)

00:26:47.283 --> 00:26:49.922
You're not fine, you're fantastic.

00:26:49.922 --> 00:26:53.792
You have life changing ideas for a reason,

00:26:53.792 --> 00:26:56.942
and it's not to torture yourself.

00:26:56.942 --> 00:26:59.063
Thank you, thank you, Doug.

00:26:59.063 --> 00:27:00.255
(audience applauding)

00:27:00.255 --> 00:27:02.644
You know, Christina's right
when she said all of you

00:27:02.644 --> 00:27:05.462
could be on stage, because all of you,

00:27:05.462 --> 00:27:07.343
we're all in this category.

00:27:07.343 --> 00:27:09.010
One in 400 trillion.

00:27:12.157 --> 00:27:16.440
All day long you have ideas
that could change your life,

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.458
that could change the world,
that could change the way

00:27:18.458 --> 00:27:21.855
that you feel, and what
do you do with them?

00:27:21.855 --> 00:27:22.688
Nothing.

00:27:23.626 --> 00:27:24.587
Hopefully I won't moon you.

00:27:24.587 --> 00:27:27.006
(audience laughing)

00:27:27.006 --> 00:27:27.965
You didn't pay for that.

00:27:27.965 --> 00:27:30.744
(intense drumming)

00:27:30.744 --> 00:27:32.139
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:27:32.139 --> 00:27:35.503
I made this video because
Francesca SugarArt asked me to.

00:27:35.503 --> 00:27:36.929
So, if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:27:36.929 --> 00:27:38.980
